 paras:[
 "Artificial intelligence is steadily entering hospitals. Algorithms can now scan thousands of medical images in minutes, flagging suspicious spots that a tired radiologist might overlook. In some trials, such systems have detected early-stage cancers with accuracy comparable to that of experienced specialists. Supporters argue that the technology will free doctors from routine tasks and give them more time with patients.",
 "Yet the enthusiasm is tempered by serious concerns. An algorithm learns from the data it is given, and if that data mostly comes from one population, its predictions may be less reliable for others. One widely used tool, for instance, systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients because it relied on past spending as a proxy for illness.",
 "Transparency is another issue. Many powerful models operate as black boxes: they produce an answer but cannot explain how they reached it. Clinicians are understandably uneasy about acting on advice they cannot scrutinize, particularly when a wrong decision could cost a life. Regulators in several countries are therefore demanding evidence that such tools are safe and fair before approving them.",
