 paras:[
 "In 2015, a team of psychologists attempted to repeat one hundred published experiments. Fewer than half of the original findings could be reproduced. The result sent a shock through the scientific community, and similar difficulties have since been reported in medicine, economics and biology. Many researchers now speak of a 'replication crisis'.",
 "Several factors contribute to the problem. Journals prefer to publish striking, positive results, so studies that find nothing tend to remain in a drawer. Researchers, who need publications to secure jobs and funding, may be tempted to analyze their data in many ways until something significant emerges. Small samples further increase the odds that a result is a fluke.",
 "In response, a movement known as open science has gained momentum. Scientists are encouraged to register their hypotheses before collecting data, so that they cannot adjust them afterwards to fit the outcome. Others share raw data and computer code, allowing colleagues to check every step of an analysis. Some journals now accept papers on the strength of their methods, regardless of what the results show.",
 "These reforms are not free of cost. Preregistration takes time, and sharing data raises privacy concerns when participants are involved. Nonetheless, many researchers believe that a slower, more transparent science is preferable to a fast one that cannot be trusted. Public confidence in research, they argue, depends on it."],
