/* ========== TEXTO 1: Self-Healing Materials ========== */
{title:"Self-Healing Materials", topic:"Engenharia · Ciência dos Materiais",
 gloss:[["Vessels","tube-like structures"],["Ruptures","breaks open"],["Polymerizes","forms a solid polymer"],["Toughness","resistance to fracture"],["Ionomeric","containing ionic groups"],["Hierarchical","organized at several scales"]],
 paras:[
 "For most of human history, materials have been designed with a single, somewhat pessimistic assumption in mind: that damage is inevitable and irreversible. A cracked bridge girder, a scratched smartphone screen, or a punctured fuel tank must be repaired externally, often at considerable expense. Nature, by contrast, operates on a different principle. A cut on the skin closes within days; a broken bone knits itself back together; even a damaged tree trunk can seal off the injury and, in some cases, regenerate lost tissue. Engineers have long envied this capacity, and over the past two decades they have begun to replicate it in synthetic materials, giving rise to a field known as self-healing materials.",
 "The biological inspiration is more than metaphorical. Living tissues repair themselves because they contain reservoirs of mobile agents—cells, proteins, and chemical signals—that can migrate to the site of damage and initiate a response. Synthetic self-healing materials borrow the same logic, embedding within a solid matrix tiny vessels or capsules filled with a healing agent. When a crack propagates and ruptures one of these containers, the liquid flows into the fissure and, upon contact with a catalyst dispersed throughout the material, polymerizes, effectively gluing the two faces of the crack together. The first widely publicized example, reported in 2001, used this approach in an epoxy composite and recovered almost seventy-five percent of its original fracture toughness after healing.",
 "Not all systems rely on embedded liquids. A second strategy exploits reversible chemical bonds—hydrogen bonds, dynamic covalent bonds, or metal-ligand interactions—that can break under stress and reassemble when the material is heated, pressed, or allowed to rest. Ionomeric polymers used in some sports equipment and coatings already exhibit this behavior, sealing small scratches at room temperature. A third approach, vascular self-healing, resembles the circulatory system of an organism: a network of hollow channels supplies healing agent continuously to any region that sustains damage, allowing multiple repairs at the same location rather than the single use permitted by a discrete capsule.",
 "The potential applications are considerable. In aerospace engineering, where a hairline crack in a composite wing panel may be undetectable during routine inspection, self-healing resins could extend service life and reduce maintenance costs. In civil infrastructure, concrete containing encapsulated bacteria or polymers could seal microcracks before water penetrates and corrodes reinforcing steel, a process that currently accounts for a substantial share of the deterioration observed in bridges and marine structures. Electronic devices, batteries, and flexible displays are also promising targets, since repeated mechanical cycling tends to produce small defects that eventually lead to failure.",
 "Significant obstacles remain, however. Healing agents are consumable; once the capsules are depleted, the material loses its ability to repair itself. Encapsulation also introduces weak points where cracks may initiate in the first place, slightly reducing the material's initial strength. Reversible-bond systems, meanwhile, often require elevated temperatures or prolonged pressure to reform, limiting their use in applications where such conditions cannot be supplied. Perhaps most importantly, the cost of manufacturing self-healing composites is currently several times that of conventional counterparts, which has restricted their adoption to specialized sectors willing to pay a premium for durability.",
 "None of these difficulties is insurmountable. Researchers are exploring recyclable healing agents, hierarchical architectures that combine multiple strategies, and manufacturing routes that may eventually drive down costs. What unites the field is a philosophical shift: instead of treating damage as a terminal event, self-healing materials treat it as an opportunity for the material to respond. If the promise is realized, the implications extend well beyond engineering, touching on questions of sustainability, resource efficiency, and the design of products that, like biological organisms, are built to endure."
