/* ========== TEXTO 2: Large Language Models and Science ========== */
{title:"Large Language Models and Scientific Discovery", topic:"Inteligência Artificial · Ciência",
 gloss:[["Corpora","collections of text"],["Tokens","units of text processed by a model"],["Weights","numerical parameters"],["Opaque","not transparent"],["Epistemic","relating to knowledge"]],
 paras:[
 "Over the past decade, artificial intelligence has moved from the periphery of scientific practice to its center. Machine learning systems now assist in tasks as varied as classifying astronomical images, predicting the properties of new chemical compounds, and recommending promising drug candidates for clinical trials. Among the most striking developments has been the emergence of large language models—systems trained on vast quantities of text that can generate fluent prose, answer questions, write software, and summarize technical material. For researchers accustomed to viewing computation as a tool for numerical analysis, these models represent something qualitatively different: an instrument that appears to reason in natural language.",
 "Large language models operate through a deceptively simple mechanism. During training, they are exposed to enormous corpora of text and learn to predict which word is most likely to follow a given sequence. Repeated over billions of parameters and trillions of tokens, this statistical exercise produces systems capable of remarkably sophisticated behavior, from translating between languages to explaining complex concepts. Crucially, however, the models do not possess an explicit database of facts or a symbolic representation of the world. Their apparent knowledge is distributed across patterns of numerical weights, and their responses are generated probabilistically rather than retrieved from a verified source. This design is the source both of their flexibility and of their limitations.",
 "Scientific applications have multiplied rapidly. In molecular biology, language models trained on protein sequences have helped predict three-dimensional structures that once required years of laboratory work to determine. In chemistry, similar systems propose synthetic routes to target molecules and flag likely side effects. In software engineering, coding assistants now draft substantial portions of the code used in research pipelines, from data cleaning to statistical analysis. Even the traditionally human task of reading the scientific literature has been partially automated, with models summarizing hundreds of papers on a topic and highlighting inconsistencies that a single researcher might miss.",
 "Yet enthusiasm must be tempered by an awareness of the risks. The most widely discussed is the tendency of these models to 'hallucinate'—to generate confident statements that are factually incorrect, sometimes fabricating citations or experimental results. Because the output is fluent and grammatically impeccable, such errors can be difficult to detect without independent verification. A second concern is interpretability: the internal computations of large models are opaque even to their designers, making it hard to explain why a particular prediction was produced. And a third is bias: models trained on historical text inherit the assumptions embedded in that text, which may systematically disadvantage certain populations or perspectives.",
 "None of these concerns implies that language models are unsuited to science, but they do indicate the kind of role they should play. The researchers most effectively using these tools describe them not as autonomous agents but as collaborators that propose hypotheses, draft text, and suggest directions which human experts then evaluate. Verification remains the responsibility of scientists, who bring domain knowledge, methodological training, and an understanding of what it means for a claim to be supported by evidence. Where this division of labor is respected, productivity gains appear real; where it is ignored, errors accumulate silently.",
 "The broader question is institutional rather than purely technical. Universities, journals, and funding agencies are only beginning to develop norms for how AI-generated content should be disclosed, evaluated, and credited. Some journals now require authors to state explicitly which tools were used and for what purposes, while others have imposed outright bans that many researchers consider impractical. What seems clear is that language models will remain part of the scientific landscape. The challenge is to integrate them in ways that preserve the epistemic standards on which science depends—transparency, reproducibility, and the willingness to be wrong."
 ],
