 "Across the sciences, a troubling pattern has emerged over the past two decades: a substantial fraction of published findings cannot be reproduced by independent researchers. Psychology, medicine, and economics have all reported replication rates below fifty percent, and the resulting debate—often called the reproducibility crisis—has prompted reforms in publication practices and statistical methods. Machine learning, despite its reputation for rigor and its reliance on code, has not escaped this problem. On the contrary, several surveys suggest that a large proportion of papers in the field cannot be fully reproduced from the information they provide, even when the underlying ideas are sound.",
 "The stakes are higher than they might first appear. Machine learning increasingly underpins systems that affect people's lives—medical diagnosis, credit scoring, hiring, criminal justice, autonomous driving—and the reliability of these systems depends on the reliability of the research that produced them. When a published model cannot be reproduced, downstream users cannot verify that it behaves as claimed, cannot compare it fairly with alternatives, and cannot build on it with confidence. Irreproducibility also wastes resources: researchers who cannot reproduce prior work must re-derive it from scratch, and promising directions may be abandoned because their foundational results turn out to be fragile.",
 "Several factors contribute to the problem. Some are computational: results may depend on undocumented implementation details, on specific random seeds, or on hyperparameters chosen through extensive and unreported search. Hardware differences—different GPUs, different versions of libraries, different numerical precision—can shift results in ways that are hard to trace. Some factors are data-related: datasets may be restricted, may change over time, or may be preprocessed in ways the paper does not describe. And some are institutional: a field that rewards novel results over careful verification creates little incentive to invest in reproducibility, and reviewers rarely have the time or resources to check a paper's claims independently.",
